WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.181 --> 00:00:03.151
(typewriter keys clacking rhythmically)

00:00:03.151 --> 00:00:05.568
(bell dings)

00:00:15.511 --> 00:00:18.256
- My name is Alex Holman, and
I play tenor Smith Corona

00:00:18.256 --> 00:00:20.370
with the Boston Typewriter Orchestra.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:21.559
The Boston Typewriter Orchestra

00:00:21.559 --> 00:00:23.837
is an ensemble for typewriter and voice.

00:00:23.837 --> 00:00:26.418
Some of us have musical
backgrounds, some of us don't.

00:00:26.418 --> 00:00:29.239
Several of our members
play in bands currently.

00:00:29.239 --> 00:00:32.687
I haven't touched much of
anything, except for a typewriter,

00:00:32.687 --> 00:00:34.901
since playing the trombone in high school.

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:36.999
We play a wide variety of typewriters,

00:00:36.999 --> 00:00:39.141
from some small, portable models

00:00:39.141 --> 00:00:43.308
all the way up to some
relatively large desk models.

00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:48.478
A lot of what we do in building
up songs is really exploring

00:00:48.478 --> 00:00:50.389
what different sounds we can
get out of the typewriter

00:00:50.389 --> 00:00:53.618
and how we can incorporate
them into a song.

00:00:53.618 --> 00:00:55.050
For example you have the key

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:58.166
makes it a basic clack sound,

00:00:58.166 --> 00:00:59.599
um, but then you have the Shift

00:00:59.599 --> 00:01:01.519
that moves a lot of the mechanics

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:03.658
and makes somewhat of a bass sound.

00:01:03.658 --> 00:01:05.879
Then you also have the roller

00:01:05.879 --> 00:01:07.959
that gets a (roller clicks),

00:01:07.959 --> 00:01:10.000
gets sort of a mechanical,
ratchet sound to it.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.527
Um, and then ultimately, we can work

00:01:11.527 --> 00:01:14.589
with the carriage return for
(return rattles)

00:01:14.589 --> 00:01:16.734
like the standard swoopy sound to it.

00:01:16.734 --> 00:01:19.254
As our name suggests, the
Boston Typewriter Orchestra

00:01:19.254 --> 00:01:21.828
is Boston-based, but
we've actually played

00:01:21.828 --> 00:01:23.552
all over New England.

00:01:23.552 --> 00:01:27.673
Most of the typewriters
are 50, 60, 70 years old.

00:01:27.673 --> 00:01:29.364
Usually in our hands we see

00:01:29.364 --> 00:01:32.134
two to three years of lifespan.

00:01:32.134 --> 00:01:34.722
We beat on them pretty hard.

00:01:34.722 --> 00:01:36.356
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:01:36.356 --> 00:01:38.476
- My fingers hurt.

00:01:38.476 --> 00:01:40.326
- When we started out in 2004,

00:01:40.326 --> 00:01:42.257
we were a bunch of guys getting together

00:01:42.257 --> 00:01:44.334
and kind of banging on typewriters.

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:45.774
We get that we're kinda weird.

00:01:45.774 --> 00:01:47.516
It's a little bit of a wacky idea,

00:01:47.516 --> 00:01:51.103
but we've discovered that this
makes some legitimate music

00:01:51.103 --> 00:01:53.446
and it can be a real serious endeavor.

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:55.526
We have true songs that come together

00:01:55.526 --> 00:01:57.554
and people seem to really enjoy them.

00:01:57.554 --> 00:01:59.653
And as long as we still
are entertaining people

00:01:59.653 --> 00:02:02.134
we're gonna stay out there, typing.

00:02:02.134 --> 00:02:04.973
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:02:04.973 --> 00:02:07.890
(electronic music)

